  "live_records": {
    "q-105633": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105633",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Calculate $∇\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F)$ for the vector field $F$ where\n\n$$\nF=x{e}^{2x}\\,i-y{e}^{2x}\\,j+x{z}^{2}\\,k.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F)=\\frac{1}{2}∇\\cdot F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y{e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}+2xz \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2x}+2xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F) & =\\frac{1}{2}∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (2x{e}^{2x}+2xz) \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
